source. A comparison is made between a solely liquid lithium blanket, figure 4(a), and a hybrid dual breeder design, figure 4(b), where lithium is contained within the first 31 layers and layer 32–50 contain FLiBe. This was chosen because it most closely represents the best performing configuration in figure 2. Firstly, when viewing the blanket which contains liquid lithium only, figure 4(a), the neutron spectral profile remains consistent throughout the depth of the blanket. A reduction in neutron flux is seen when comparing the first and final layers, though this is across all energies in an almost uniform fashion. When comparing this with the spec- tral profiles found in figure 4(b), a clear change is seen in two places: within all FLiBe layers and within the lithium layer dir- ectly in front of the FLiBe (layers 18 - 31). In both cases the population of lower energy neutrons is increased, and espe- cially in the layers containing FLiBe this extends into the near thermal neutron region. This shift to lower energy neutrons within the FLiBe layers is due to the greater moderating power of the FLiBe (particularly Be) [43], while the shift within the lithium layer can be explained due to the presence of the FLiBe layer back scattering the now moderated neutrons towards the lithium. The TBR per layer was also measured, found in 4(c), where a comparison between the liquid lithium blanket and the hybrid design is seen. Again as shown in figure 3 an increase in the hybrid design is seen in the preceding lithium layers, extending for around 13 layers, which can now be explained due to the higher populations of lower energy neutrons. As pre- viously mentioned the cross section for the 6Li(n,T) grows as a power law (at energies lower than approximately 100 KeV) so any increase in neutrons at lower energies should provide greater probability of tritium breeding. The final TBR increase can be seen in the initial layers of FLiBe (layers 32–36) alternatively this is due the 9Be(n,2n) reaction and provides a small increase in the neutron populations, as shown in figure 4(d). 6

